## 6. Task 5 — New API endpoints (`apps/engine/app/routers/live.py`, new file, register in `main.py` alongside the existing routers)

- `POST /bots/{bot_id}/live/start` — body: `{ symbol: str, capital: float }`. Validates bot ownership (same `get_current_user_id` dependency pattern as every other authenticated route), validates the bot's graph via the existing `validate_bot_graph` (refuse to start if it has blocking errors — reuse, don't reimplement), creates the `live_sessions` row (relying on the partial unique index to reject a second concurrent session for the same bot — catch the resulting IntegrityError and return a clean 409), updates `bots.status = 'live'`, and registers the scheduler job (Task 4). Returns the new session id.
- `POST /bots/{bot_id}/live/stop` — the kill switch. Sets the active session's `status = 'stopped'`, `stopped_at = now()`, updates `bots.status = 'paused'`, and removes the scheduler job (`scheduler.remove_job(f"live-{bot_id}")`, guarded — job may not exist if the engine restarted).
- `GET /bots/{bot_id}/live/state` — returns the current session (or 404/`{status: 'not_running'}` if none active), its `position`, latest N (e.g. 200) `live_equity_points`, and latest N (e.g. 50) `live_trades`, in the same field-naming convention (`camelCase`) as `GET /backtest/{run_id}` already uses, so the frontend can reuse formatting helpers.

All three endpoints must reject with 403/404 if the requesting user doesn't own the bot — same ownership-check pattern already established in `backtest.py`.

